{"name":"Milk Jug","hex":"#faf7f0","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"milk jug","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A jug for holding milk.","A woman's breast."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/milk_jug"}],"entries":[{"title":"Plastic milk container","blocks":[{"text":"Plastic milk containers are plastic containers for storing, shipping and dispensing milk. Plastic bottles, sometimes called jugs, have largely replaced glass bottles for home consumption. Glass milk bottles have traditionally been reusable while light-weight plastic bottles are designed for single trips and plastic recycling."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9c/Bottle_of_milk.jpg/330px-Bottle_of_milk.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plastic_milk_container","wikidata":"Q55630103"},{"title":"Milk","blocks":[{"text":"Milk is usually a white liquid food produced by the mammary glands of lactating mammals. It is the primary source of nutrition for young mammals before they are able to digest solid food. Milk contains many nutrients, including calcium and protein, as well as lactose and saturated fat; the enzyme lactase is needed to break down lactose. Immune factors and immune-modulating components in milk contribute to milk immunity. The first milk, which is called colostrum, contains antibodies and immune-modulating components that strengthen the immune system against many diseases."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Glass_of_Milk_%2833657535532%29.jpg/330px-Glass_of_Milk_%2833657535532%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Milk","wikidata":"Q8495"},{"title":"Jug","blocks":[{"text":"A jug is a type of container commonly used to hold and serve liquids, but not normally to drink from directly. It has an opening, sometimes narrow, from which to pour or drink, and has a handle, and usually a pouring lip. Jugs throughout history have been made of metal, ceramic, or glass, and plastic is now common."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/M0354_990116-6_1_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-M0354_990116-6_1_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jug","wikidata":"Q2413314"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/Borie_del_s%C3%A8rre.JPG/500px-Borie_del_s%C3%A8rre.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/Borie_del_s%C3%A8rre.JPG/1280px-Borie_del_s%C3%A8rre.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/Borie_del_s%C3%A8rre.JPG/500px-Borie_del_s%C3%A8rre.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Plastic bottles with indentations for handles","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABorie_del_s%C3%A8rre.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/VolumeAdjuster.jpg/500px-VolumeAdjuster.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/VolumeAdjuster.jpg/1280px-VolumeAdjuster.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/VolumeAdjuster.jpg/500px-VolumeAdjuster.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The indentation on the left side of a milk bottle adjusts the volume of the bottle.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVolumeAdjuster.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/Coffee_fresh_%282012.08.04%29.jpg/120px-Coffee_fresh_%282012.08.04%29.jpg?utm_source=